  clear snapshot of the molecular  differences in the tumor  microenvironment between a rare form of  breast cancer known as invasive globular  carcinoma or ilc  anatomically ilc  is a type of again a rare form of breast  cancer that develops within the milk  producing lobules of the breast a more  common type of breast cancer is invasive  ducts of carcinoma which develops in the  ducts of the breast which they do in  this paper compare the molecular  signatures between these two subtypes  so here just shows a schematic of their  papers what they did was take 23 fresh  frozen  invasive globular carcinoma samples then  used laser capture micro dissection with  one of our arcturus instruments to  separate the stroma populations  from the tumor epithelium  they were primarily focused on  cancer-associated fibroblasts so these  cells were the targets of their stromal  microdissection  they then isolated rna from each  population of cells and performed  microarray analysis to look at gene  expression patterns across the samples  i did want to highlight one of their  supplementary figures  as they utilize the camera on the  arcturus instrument to document the  laser capture process which is a great  feature for when you're looking to  publish your data generated from this  method  so here you see a couple different  samples on the left is a sample that  they are isolating out the tumor  epithelium so you can see the original  sample on top  the middle picture shows the sample  after dissection  and then the bottom shows what was  captured onto the capture cap  similarly on the right you see a sample  of the isolated tumor stroma from and  again you can see all three stages  documented using that onboard instrument  camera  now to look at a little bit of the data  that they generated using this process  if you want to see all of their data i  highly recommend going to read this  whole paper as well  so now they have their two distinct  populations that they've dissected out  and separated the tumor epithelium and  the tumor stroma  like i said they performed an rna  microarray to look at the differential  gene expression among the populations  which is depicted in this heat map here  um they found over a thousand genes that  were significantly highly expressed in  the stromal population and 837 genes in  the tumor epithelium  and when they looked at specific  molecular pathways that these genes were  involved in they found that for the  stromal compartment  they saw an upregulation of genes  involved in ecm remodeling and cell  surface interactions while the tumor  epithelium showed  up regulation of genes development cell  cycle progression dna replication  epigenetic modifications  so before i wrap up i did want to touch  on a really neat specialized application  that we support on the arcturus  instrument that is relevant to oncology  and that is lifestyle micro dissection  using our micro dissection growth  chambers  so i know the first paper i discussed  today did a lifestyle dissection they  used a frame membrane slide as the  growth chamber so another option that we  have is using this petri dish system  that has two parts  so the first is this growth chamber  where the bottom is entirely made up of  pen membrane so that same pen membrane  that's used on those slides  the second piece is the micro dissection  petri dish this bottom is solid but it's  coated with silicone  and the way the system works is you  place your growth chamber  in a standard tissue culture plate and  you grow your cells like normal  when you've either reached some fluency  or whatever end point you're looking for  you remove that growth chamber and place  it into the sterile micro dissection  petri dish  so those two together are placed into  are placed onto the instrument with a  specific petri dish stage insert which  is included with the instrument  and to dissect with this system you  utilize the uv laser so you cut around  your cells of interest which releases  those cells from the growth chamber and  sticks them to the silicone coated  bottom of the petri dish  so when you're finished and you separate  out those two elements you're left with  two separate populations the ones you've  dissected which are now on the bottom of  that petri dish and the ones that are  left behind which are still in the  growth chamber  so from there you can actually  trypsinize and reculture either  population of cells and it does not  affect nucleic acid viability as we  showed here  so this is an example that comes from  one of our white papers um showing that  um  showing cells that were micro dissected  um using the system are able to be uh  re-cultured and even 48 hours post-micro  dissection we can still isolate out good  quality rna as shown here by these  bio-analyzer traces  as cell culture is such a vital tool in  oncology research  as i know it was for me  this vastly opens the possibilities that  lcm can be used for in your specific  research workflow  and when it comes to documenting your  micro dissection process we again make  it very easy and straightforward to take  pictures throughout using that built-in  camera system  so you can see here we have a variety of  tissues and stain types that can be  micro dissected  where a picture was taken prior to micro  dissection which is this top row  which i do want to add you can actually  take pictures that have your drawings  overlaid on your tissue so you can see  where you've marked up cells that you're  interested in  micro dissecting  then you can show the tissue after micro  dissection to demonstrate what was  actually picked up  and then of course with the quality  control station on the instrument you  can take pictures of the bottom of the  caps themselves to show the cells that  were indeed micro dissected  you also have the flexibility of  document using video if that's something  you need for publication or maybe  teaching purposes  all right so wrapping up with a brief  summary of what i've covered today um  first we discussed uh the lcm technology  using the arcturus select  which with its dual laser capability  allows for the flexibility of types of  tissues that can be micro dissected do  hard tissue like bone soft delicate  tissue they're both doable with this  type of system  we also add flexibility to the workflow  through our various types of caps and  slides so that we can meet your specific  experimental needs  we also of course heavily focused on how  lcm can be used specifically in oncology  research as it's a great way to tease  apart differences within the tumor  whether you're addressing tumor  heterogeneity to your micro environment  or both  and finally at thermal fissure we're  able to provide you with everything you  might need for the workflow from  consumables staining kits extraction  kits and even genomic analysis  instruments  and if you're looking for more lcm  resources from us you can visit the  laser capture micro dissection section  on our website at thermofisher.com um  where you can get an overview of the  select system introductions to the  workflow where to order consumables and  we even have a  resource page specific to oncology  research  i highly recommend taking a look around  that page or reaching out to us for any  specific questions you may have  regarding your particular research  applications  so thank you so much for joining me  today listening to me speak on this  topic and i hope you found that useful  and that some of you are considering  utilizing this really neat technology to  further your oncology research  thank you dr walter for your informative  presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar if you have a question  you'd like to ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  let's get started our first question is  can high sensitivity